  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose to use a hierarchical optimization strategy that combines spectral methods and non-convex optimization to find a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. The core idea is to represent the step function in both frequency and time domains to escape local minima. This approach addresses the main bottleneck in previous methods\u2014local optima\u2014by:\n- Leveraging the spectral decomposition to balance frequency components\n- Maintaining a population of candidate solutions to explore diverse areas\n- Converting the continuous optimization space into a discrete representation for better control\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   We optimize over a piecewise constant function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with exactly n samples (n must be even). The objective is:\n   minimize C_5 = (1/dx) * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   subject to:\n   \u2211h_i = n/2, and 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n   where dx = 2/n\n\n   We approximate the integrals with Riemann sums. The spectral decomposition is used to:\n   \u2022 Represent h in the frequency domain as \u2131(h)\n   \u2022 Project the overlap scores onto the dominant frequency components\n   \u2022 Apply Tikhonov regularization to prevent overfitting\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   Stage 1: Initialization (time: 5% of budget)\n   - Start with four distinct initializations (original, left-right shift, top-bottom shift, random perturbation)\n   - Clip all solutions to satisfy feasibility constraints\n   - Automatically determine optimal number of resolution levels (n) based on problem size\n\n   Stage 2: Spectral Analysis & Projection (time: 30% of budget)\n   - Compute frequency profiles for all candidates\n   - Identify dominant frequency components responsible for high overlap\n   - For each candidate, adjust amplitude distribution based on spectral gaps\n\n   Stage 3: Population-Based Optimization (time: 40% of budget)\n   - Maintain 30-50 elite solutions across resolution levels\n   - Use a dynamic chromosome representation where each position corresponds to a sample point\n   - Implement adaptive crossover rate based on spectral gap scores\n   - Apply endpoint anchoring to maintain integral constraint (sum(h)=n/2)\n\n   Stage 4: Refinement & Multi-resolution Processing (time: 25% of budget)\n   - Perform multi-frequency smoothing using inverse filter banks\n   -Explore both increasing and decreasing resolution paths\n   -Integrate spectral peaks via dynamic programming\n\n   4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Population size: [30,80] defaults to 50\n   - Mutation rate: adaptive (initial: 0.05 - 0.25\n   - Resolution evolution: exponentially increase from base level by factor of 2-1.5 each iteration\n   - Chromosome flipping probability: 20-40%\n   - \u2022 Sigmoid crossover for balance coefficients: lerp between 0.2-0.8\n\n   5. Budget and Robustness\n   - Dominant cost factors: spectral decomposition (\u223c40%), population evolution (\u223c35%)\n   - Phase allocation: \n     - Initial: 100s (any remaining time scales proportionally)\n     - Initialization: 15%\n     - Spectral analysis: 35%\n     - Population-based: 45%\n     - Multi-resolution: 5%\n   - Fallback strategy: Return parent solution if any phase fails\n\n   6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Post-run verification:\n     - h[i] must be in [0,1] for all i\n     - sum(h) * dx = 1\n     - Re-compute C5 using the provided evaluate_erdos_solution() method\n     - Score robustness checks: 3 independent recomputations\n   - Output structure:\n     - h_values: numpy array of feasible solution\n     - c5_bound: validated overlap score\n     - n_points: even integer determined during resolution evolution",
